1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,462
The Secret of Gecko Feet.

2
00:00:02,412 --> 00:00:15,362
The extraordinary climbing ability of geckos — their capacity to adhere to and detach from vertical surfaces, including smooth glass — results from a hierarchical system of microscopic structures on their toe pads.

3
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:27,537
Each toe pad is covered in rows of setae, hair-like projections approximately 100 micrometres in length, which branch into hundreds of even smaller spatulae, each roughly 200 nanometres across.

4
00:00:27,537 --> 00:00:36,475
This branched architecture creates an enormous contact area: a single gecko's setae generate over 20 million spatulae in contact with a surface.

5
00:00:36,475 --> 00:00:45,025
The adhesive mechanism is van der Waals force — weak intermolecular attractions arising from temporary fluctuations in electron distribution.

6
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:53,175
Though individually minuscule, the cumulative van der Waals forces across millions of spatulae produce strong, reversible adhesion.

7
00:00:53,175 --> 00:01:04,375
A single seta, as demonstrated by Autumn and colleagues in a 2002 Nature paper, generates approximately 10 micronewtons of adhesive force — enough to support a small ant.

8
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:14,775
The millions of setae on a tokay gecko (Gekko gecko) collectively produce enough adhesion to hold 40 kilograms, over 100 times the animal's own body weight.

9
00:01:14,775 --> 00:01:17,900
Self-cleaning is another remarkable property.

10
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:25,950
Gecko setae shed dirt particles through contact with surfaces, as contaminants adhere more strongly to the surface than to the toe pads.

11
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:34,450
Biomimetic researchers have developed synthetic adhesives inspired by gecko feet, including climbing robots and surgical adhesives.

12
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:46,212
A 2015 study in the Journal of Experimental Biology tested a gecko-inspired climbing pad with hierarchical microstructures that achieved shear adhesion of 10 kPa on smooth surfaces.

